In modern MSTest (3.6+), use **constructor injection** for `TestContext` and synchronous setup, then `[TestInitialize]` only for async work:

```csharp
[TestClass]
public sealed class MyServiceTests
{
    private readonly TestContext _testContext;
    private readonly MyService _service;

    public MyServiceTests(TestContext testContext)
    {
        _testContext = testContext;
        _service = new MyService(); // sync init in constructor → readonly fields
    }

    [TestInitialize]
    public async Task InitAsync()
    {
        // async-only setup here
        await _service.ConnectAsync(_testContext.CancellationToken);
    }

    [TestCleanup]
    public async Task CleanupAsync()
    {
        await _service.DisconnectAsync();
    }

    [TestMethod]
    [Timeout(5000)]
    public async Task DoWork_ReturnsExpected()
    {
        var result = await _service.DoWorkAsync(_testContext.CancellationToken);
        Assert.IsNotNull(result);
    }
}
```

**Key points:**

- Constructor runs first, receives `TestContext` automatically — no property needed.
- Put all sync setup in the constructor so fields can be `readonly`.
- Use `[TestInitialize]` **only** when you need `await`.
- Flow `_testContext.CancellationToken` into async calls to respect `[Timeout]`.
